      "excerpts": [
        "AAV infection is initiated by the binding of the primary receptor on the host cell. The cell membrane undergoes a conformational change in the presence of clathrin-associated proteins to form the early endosome (pH 6.0). The early endosome is acidified and forms the late endosome (pH 5.5) and the lysosome (pH 4.0).\nAAV cellular trafficking is initiated by the interaction of the virus capsid with the primary receptor displayed on the target host cell. AAVs predominantly utilize glycans as the primary receptors and proteins as their secondary receptors, for example integrin and laminin.\n8 , 9 , 10 Once the capsid binds to the cellular receptors, three pathways have been proposed to facilitate its endocytosis into the cellular milieu: (1) the clathrin-independent carriers (caveolae) and GPI-enriched endocytic compartment (CLIC/GEEC) and AAVR, (2) macropinocytosis, and (3) clathrin-mediated endocytosis and the trans -Golgi network\nThe most widely accepted pathway is the clathrin-mediated endocytosis of the AAV, followed by the acidification of the early endosome (pH 6) and the late endosome (pH 5.5).\nThe low endosomal pH of the late endosome facilitates the externalization of VP1u, which contains a PLA 2 domain that has been proposed to cleave the endosome and allow the virus to escape into the cellular matrix. 13 , 14 Recently, it has also been suggested that the trafficking of the virus occurs via the TGN, 15 which hosts both GPR108 and AAVR.\nSimilar data were obtained for cell-to-cell barrier functions with only actin staining ( Figure 4 Bv and x) for reproducibility. AAV2 utilizes heparan sulfate proteoglycan as its primary receptor and multiple secondary receptors to facilitate recognition and binding of the target tissue, which have been extensively reviewed.\nAs shown previously and in this study, the trafficking of AAV2 in cells is initiated by receptor-mediated endocytosis. 11 , 40 , 41 , 49 , 48 , 50 , 51 The known primary receptor of AAV2 is heparin sulfate proteoglycan, and secondary receptors include AAVR and integrin.\n24 Amino acid substitution analysis of residues D561–E565 to alanines was shown to be defective for receptor binding and infectivity. 78 Additionally, the AAV2-E563A capsid has been previously characterized and shown to be defective for infectivity 77 ; however, the trafficking phenotype of AAV2-E563A capsid is still not known.\nIn summary, this study provided a spatiotemporal view of AAV trafficking, from receptor binding and endocytosis to cytoplasmic transport via microtubules and/or actin, overcoming tight junction linkers at the plasma membrane and pH-related barriers in the endosome, nuclear targeting, and euchromatin association in the nucleus.\nThe immunofluorescence microscopy was performed to determine the AAV2 intracellular trafficking and localization in both the cytoplasmic and nuclear compartments at certain post-infection time point in HeLa cells."
      ]
